# Appendix A — Landing Page Wireframe + Full Copy

## Purpose and conversion objective

Build one dedicated, long-form landing-page system on `ergoworksconsulting.com.au`, with three message-matched variants:

1. Manual Handling Training
2. Workstation / Ergonomic Assessment
3. Hybrid / Home-office Ergonomics

These pages replace the generic WordPress destinations currently receiving paid traffic. The business case for the dedicated LP rests on **message match, offer and form — not speed**: the live site is already fast (Lighthouse 97 mobile on the manual-handling page, LCP 2.0s, CLS 0, per `research/live-evidence-2026-07-12.md` §Lighthouse), so the new page must merely not regress below those thresholds. The system supports the existing mature Google Search program—approximately **A$40,000 trailing 12 months**, with an observed range of **A$3.0k–A$4.7k per month** and approximately **20 real leads per month**—and the proposed Meta demand-generation activity.

The commercial objective is **cost per qualified lead**, progressing to cost per held consultation and pipeline generated. Newsletter sign-ups, unqualified form completions and other soft actions are not success metrics.

The launch-default dominant CTA is the **free 15-minute Workplace Ergonomics Risk Snapshot** for organisations. It covers one defined workplace question and provides a written three-point summary. **It is preliminary guidance only—not a compliance assessment, compliance certification, medical assessment or diagnosis.** Service-plan-primary variants are post-baseline tests only.

## Modular wireframe

All three variants use the same modular page structure. Only the hero, benefit emphasis, process language, proof selection, FAQs and form defaults change.

| Order | Module | Purpose | Required execution |
|---:|---|---|---|
| 1 | Stripped header | Establish legitimacy without creating exits | ErgoWorks logo, “Australia-wide workplace ergonomics” — **client sign-off required (see geography launch blocker)**, optional clickable phone number and one primary CTA. No full navigation, service dropdowns, store links or physiotherapy links. |
| 2 | Hero | Confirm ad relevance immediately | Exact service phrase in the H1, outcome-led subhead, one dominant CTA, short qualification line and relevant workplace image. |
| 3 | Proof strip | Reduce initial uncertainty | Maximum three verified signals. Every proposed proof asset remains marked **client sign-off required** until substantiated and approved. |
| 4 | Benefit blocks | Explain controllable business outputs | Three concise blocks covering visibility, prioritisation and implementation—not guaranteed health, injury, compliance or claims outcomes. |
| 5 | How it works | Make delivery concrete | Three or four steps with clear inputs, activities and buyer deliverables. |
| 6 | Snapshot offer | Provide a low-commitment next step | State scope, recipient, output and limitation. Always display: **Preliminary guidance only—not a compliance assessment, compliance certification, medical assessment or diagnosis.** |
| 7 | Service detail | Support buyers who need more evidence | Variant-specific delivery formats, likely outputs and suitable organisational use cases. |
| 8 | Proof section | Answer vendor-risk objections | Consultant credentials, approved examples, sample deliverables and testimonials only after verification and permission. |
| 9 | FAQ | Resolve commercial and operational objections | Cover scope, locations, delivery format, equipment independence, privacy and the limits of the Snapshot. |
| 10 | Final CTA and form | Convert evidence-seeking visitors | Repeat the same dominant CTA, embed the two-step form and show what happens next. |
| 11 | Minimal footer | Provide necessary legal and contact information | Privacy policy, terms, business contact details and consent-management link. No cross-sell to ErgoEquip or Biosymm. |

### Page-level rules

- One page variant per intent cluster; do not present training, assessment, software, physiotherapy and equipment as competing actions.
- Use the exact service phrase from the ad in the H1 and naturally within the first screen.
- Match the ad’s CTA to the landing-page CTA. Target the skill’s **100% message-match standard**: headline, offer and CTA aligned.
- Keep the Snapshot visibly B2B: use “organisation”, “workplace”, “WHS”, “HR”, “Facilities” and “team-size band”.
- Keep the clickable phone number secondary to the form CTA.
- Do not link to ErgoEquip or Biosymm. Consulting analytics, pixels, GA4 stream and GTM scope must remain isolated from those businesses and from Physiotherapy activity in the shared Ads account.
- Avoid health or injury narratives, personal-attribute messaging and open text boxes that invite medical information.
- Do not claim that the service guarantees injury prevention, ensures WHS compliance, eliminates absenteeism or produces a stated reduction in claims.
- Use only controllable promises: defined scope, task review, practical training, documented observations, prioritised actions, manager guidance and implementation tracking.
- Show a CMP without covering the headline, CTA or form. Consent controls must not delay form interaction by more than one second.
- AU privacy review, CMP and consent implementation are pre-launch gates.
- **All LP variants ship `noindex,follow` with a canonical to the closest existing service page.** Organic is negligible for this domain (15 keywords, ~60 visits/month), so there is no SEO upside — and indexing the LP risks cannibalising the one organic asset that matters: a blog post ranking **#1 for "manual handling certificate"** (`research/live-evidence-2026-07-12.md` §Ahrefs). The LP must not chase SEO; index only if the client later retires that blog post.
- **Geography claim is a LAUNCH BLOCKER, not a passive sign-off item.** The live site commits to Sydney and Melbourne only, while this appendix's header/proof strip says "Australia-wide … client sign-off required" and search terms span Canberra, Sydney, Brisbane and national terms. The only converting geo-specific term is `ergonomic office fyshwick` (the Sydney/Melbourne/Brisbane/near-me geo terms appear in the pull but did not convert). The LP cannot claim broader coverage than the live site until verified; if coverage IS Sydney/Melbourne-only, the Canberra spend needs a geo decision, not just copy (see open-questions.md, service-geography question).

